{"command": "cd /testbed && cat -n /testbed/specs/001-transactions/spec.md | head -77", "exit_code": 0, "stderr": "", "stdout": "     1\t# Feature Specification: TaggedCache with LRU Eviction\n     2\t\n     3\t**Feature Branch**: `001-tagged-cache`\n     4\t\n     5\t**Created**: 2026-09-18\n     6\t\n     7\t**Status**: Draft\n     8\t\n     9\t**Input**: User description: \"Build cachetools.tagged.TaggedCache(maxsize), using the existing cachetools.LRUCache for bounded storage and preserving all existing cachetools APIs.\"\n    10\t\n    11\t## User Scenarios & Testing\n    12\t\n    13\t### User Story 1 - Tagged Entry Insertion and Retrieval (Priority: P1)\n    14\t\n    15\tA cache consumer needs to store entries with tags for later group-based invalidation. The consumer puts entries with keys, values, and a set of string tags. When retrieving an entry by key, the consumer expects to receive a detached deep copy of the stored value. If the key does not exist, a KeyError is raised.\n    16\t\n    17\t**Why this priority**: This is the core functionality — without put/get, the TaggedCache cannot serve its purpose.\n    18\t\n    19\t**Independent Test**: Create a TaggedCache, call put with tags, call get and verify the returned value matches the stored value and is a deep copy.\n    20\t\n    21\t**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n    22\t\n    23\t1. **Given** an empty TaggedCache(maxsize=2), **When** I call `c.put('a', 1, ['x'])`, **Then** the entry is stored with key 'a', value 1, and tag 'x'.\n    24\t2. **Given** a cache with `c.put('a', [1, 2], ['x'])`, **When** I call `val = c.get('a')`, **Then** `val` equals `[1, 2]` and `val is not [1, 2]` (deep copy).\n    25\t3. **Given** a cache without key 'missing', **When** I call `c.get('missing')`, **Then** a KeyError is raised.\n    26\t4. **Given** a cache with `c.put('a', obj)`, **When** the value `obj` is mutated after put, **Then** `c.get('a')` still returns the original value (input is copied).\n    27\t\n    28\t---\n    29\t\n    30\t### User Story 2 - Tag-Based Invalidation (Priority: P1)\n    31\t\n    32\tA cache consumer needs to remove all entries carrying a specific tag. The `invalidate(tag)` method removes exactly the entries that carry that tag and returns the count of removed entries. Replacing an existing key must remove its old tag memberships before assigning new ones.\n    33\t\n    34\t**Why this priority**: Tag-based invalidation is the distinguishing feature of TaggedCache versus plain LRUCache.\n    35\t\n    36\t**Independent Test**: Insert entries with overlapping tags, invalidate a tag, verify only matching entries are removed and the return count is correct.\n    37\t\n    38\t**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n    39\t\n    40\t1. **Given** `c.put('a', 1, ['x'])` and `c.put('b', 2, ['x'])`, **When** I call `removed = c.invalidate('x')`, **Then** `removed == 2` and `len(c) == 0`.\n    41\t2. **Given** `c.put('a', 1, ['old'])` then `c.put('a', 2, ['new'])`, **When** I call `c.invalidate('old')`, **Then** `removed == 0` (old tags are gone) and `c.get('a') == 2`.\n    42\t3. **Given** a cache with entries tagged 'x' and 'y', **When** I call `c.invalidate('z')` (no matching tag), **Then** `removed == 0` and no entries are removed.\n    43\t\n    44\t---\n    45\t\n    46\t### User Story 3 - LRU Eviction with Tag Integrity (Priority: P2)\n    47\t\n    48\tA cache consumer expects that when the cache reaches maxsize and a new entry is inserted, the least-recently-used entry is evicted. Eviction must not leave stale tag memberships that affect later operations. The `get(key)` call refreshes the LRU recency of the entry.\n    49\t\n    50\t**Why this priority**: Eviction is fundamental to bounded caches; tag integrity during eviction ensures correctness of invalidate operations.\n    51\t\n    52\t**Independent Test**: Fill cache to maxsize, insert a new entry, verify the LRU entry is evicted and its tags no longer appear in invalidate results.\n    53\t\n    54\t**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n    55\t\n    56\t1. **Given** `c = TaggedCache(2)`, `c.put('a', 1, ['x'])`, `c.put('b', 2, ['y'])`, **When** I call `c.put('c', 3, ['z'])`, **Then** the LRU entry ('a') is evicted and `len(c) == 2`.\n    57\t2. **Given** the same scenario, **When** I call `c.invalidate('x')`, **Then** `removed == 0` (evicted entry's tag is not stale).\n    58\t3. **Given** a cache with entries, **When** I call `c.get('existing_key')`, **Then** the entry's LRU recency is updated (subsequent eviction evicts a different entry).\n    59\t4. **Given** `len(c)` after operations, **Then** it returns the count of live entries only.\n    60\t\n    61\t---\n    62\t\n    63\t### User Story 4 - Input Validation (Priority: P2)\n    64\t\n    65\tA cache consumer may pass invalid arguments to put. The TaggedCache must validate tags before modifying any state. Tags must be a finite iterable of strings. A bare string passed as tags must be rejected with ValueError. Non-string tag entries must be rejected with ValueError. The cache must remain unchanged after a validation failure.\n    66\t\n    67\t**Why this priority**: Validation protects the cache from corruption and ensures API contract safety.\n    68\t\n    69\t**Independent Test**: Call put with a bare string as tags, verify ValueError is raised and the cache is unchanged.\n    70\t\n    71\t**Acceptance Scenarios**:\n    72\t\n    73\t1. **Given** a TaggedCache, **When** I call `c.put('a', 1, 'x')` (bare string), **Then** ValueError is raised and the cache is unchanged.\n    74\t2. **Given** a TaggedCache, **When** I call `c.put('a', 1, ['x', 42])` (non-string entry), **Then** ValueError is raised and the cache is unchanged.\n    75\t3. **Given** a TaggedCache, **When** I call `c.put('a', 1, ['x', 'x'])` (duplicate tags), **Then** tags are deduplicated to `['x']` and the entry is stored.\n    76\t\n    77\t---\n"}